315 0 obj
<</Type/Annot/Subtype/Widget/AP<</N 703 0 R>>/BS<</S/S/W 1>>/DA(0 0 0 rg /Helv 0 Tf)/F 4/FT/Tx/Ff 8392704/M(D:20250930132529-06'00')/MK<<>>/NM(141a6788-b002-4793-8d8c-ddc790ff63ba)/Rect[66.4811 71.95642 554.681 567.478]/T(Text Field23)/V(On 04Sep26, the sample vials for ETX-260902-0505 were received from the Sample Submissions team and brought into the Sterile Microbiology lab. Upon arrival, each sample vial was sprayed with an acidified bleach disinfectant, placed into pre-disinfected bins, and allowed a 10-minute contact time.\r \rThe secondary disinfection happened in the ISO 8 anteroom \(114\), where the vials were again treated with acidified bleach and provided a 10-minute contact time before processing. Subsequently, the vials were moved into innermost ISO 7 cleanroom \(114B\). Inside this cleanroom, the processing analyst, Alex Saravia, performed a final disinfection step, allowing an additional 10-minute contact time. Once fully disinfected, the vials were transferred into the ISO 5 BSC E001316.\r \rInside the BSC, each container was aseptically opened, ensuring minimal exposure to the environment. The contents were then dispensed directly from the container into sterile canisters placed on a vacuum manifold for membrane filtration \(45 gm filtered per media across 3 vials\). The sample was processed and tested as per MICRO-SOP-5 \(USP <71> / EP 2.6.1 Sterility Test\). Media bottles were transferred into incubators E001356 and E001357 to begin incubation.\r \rOn 10Sep26 \(Day 6 of incubation\), microbial growth was observed in one TSB \(Tryptic Soy Broth\) media bottle by analyst, Elysse Nioupin, and confirmed by Microbiology Lab Supervisor, Robin Seymour. The positive TSB bottle for ETX-260902-0505 was submitted for Differential Staining and Microbial Identification under ETX-260910-0290. Differential staining indicated the presence of Gram-positive rods. Definitive identification identified the isolate as Microbacterium sp. PM5 \(Gram-positive rods\).\r \rThe culture media utilized were within their expiry period. Negative control media bottles for the membrane filtration method were handled, processed, and incubated in the same manner as the test samples. No microbial growth was observed in the corresponding negative controls.\r \rEnvironmental Monitoring \(EM\) Recoveries Review:\r \rISO 5 BSC and Personnel Evaluation:\rUpon reviewing the environmental monitoring data associated with the sterility test, no microbial growth was detected on personnel monitoring plates \(left- and right-hand touch plates for analyst Alex Saravia\), settling plates, or ISO 5 BSC surface sampling plates within BSC E001316 on the date of testing \(04Sep26\), the preceding testing date \(03Sep26\), or the subsequent testing date \(08Sep26\). Therefore, the contemporaneous personnel and ISO 5 environmental monitoring results do not support processing personnel or the ISO 5 BSC as a potential source of the Microbacterium sp. PM5 detected in test sample ETX-260902-0505.\r \rCleanroom EM Evaluation:\rRoutine weekly surface sampling of Cleanroom Suite 114 performed on 04Sep26 demonstrated no microbial growth. Weekly active-air monitoring of the outermost ISO 8 Room 114 \(CR 114\) recovered 2 CFUs \(ETX-260914-0487\), identified as Corynebacterium ureicelerivorans and Mycobacterium grossiae. All of these cleanroom environmental isolates differed distinctly in genus and morphology from the product contamination isolate \(Microbacterium sp. PM5\). Considering the facility's layered disinfection program, controlled unidirectional airflow, physical segregation of the ISO 8 anteroom, and complete absence of contamination in the ISO 5 critical zone, these recoveries represent an isolated background event and are not considered a credible source of contamination for the test sample.\r \rThe absence of contamination on analyst glove plates and work-surface environmental monitoring plates collected on the date of testing indicates that there was no viable transfer of contaminants to the ISO 5 BSC. Additionally, the lack of contamination in other samples processed during the same testing session further demonstrates that the testing environment was operating in optimal condition.\r \rThe analysts confirmed full compliance with cleaning procedures as outlined in MICRO SOP-9 \(Cleaning and Disinfecting Procedure for Microbiology\) and MICRO-SOP-5 \(USP <71> / EP 2.6.1 Sterility Test\).\r \rMonthly cleaning and disinfection of the outermost ISO 8 Anteroom, the middle ISO 7 Buffer room, the innermost ISO 7 cleanroom, and its containing ISO 5 BSCs for Cleanroom suite CR114 was performed on 30Aug26, as per MICRO-SOP-9 \(Cleaning and Disinfecting Procedure for Microbiology\). During both cleaning cycles, it was documented that all H2O2 indicators passed. This confirms the efficient monthly cleaning of all three parts of Cleanroom suite CR114.\r \rAnalyzing a 6-month sample history for Solyn LLC \(E75000\), this specific analyte "GLP3R/Cagrilinitide" has had no prior failures using USP <71> / EP 2.6.1 Sterility Test during this period.\r \rTo assess the potential for sample-to-sample contamination contributing to the positive results, a comprehensive review was conducted of all samples processed on the same day. All other samples processed by the analyst and other analysts that day tested negative. These findings suggest that cross-contamination between samples is highly unlikely.\r \rBased on the observations outlined above, it is unlikely that the failing results were due to reagents, supplies, the cleanroom environment, the process, or analyst involvement. Consequently, the possibility of laboratory error contributing to this failure is minimal and the original result is deemed to be valid.\r \rHowever, when investigating the lot history, it was discovered that the same lot \(Lot 2608-216\) had previously been submitted for ScanRDI sterility testing under ETX-260807-0602 and yielded a passing result. Based on this conflicting passing ScanRDI result for the same lot, the laboratory requested additional sample vials from the client for retesting under ETX-260914-0470 and initiated a Phase II investigation \(CORP-FORM-22\).)>>
endobj

