313 0 obj
<</Ff 8392704/BS <</S/S/W 1>>/T(Text Field22)/Rect[66.808 66.724 554.681 369.269]/V(All the analysts involved in the testing - the initial prepping analyst \(Ishita Sharma\), the initial processing analyst \(Alex Saravia\), the initial reading analyst \(Elysse Nioupin\), the retest prepping and reading analyst \(Andrew Carrillo\), and the retest processing analyst \(Ayomide Odugbesi\) - were interviewed comprehensively. Their answers are recorded throughout this document. This investigation was performed in accordance with MICRO-SOP-53, Sterility Test Out-of-Specification Investigation Procedure.\r \rINITIAL TEST UNDER SUBMISSION ETX-260902-0505:\rAll analysts involved in the prepping, processing, and reading of the sample - Ishita Sharma, Alex Saravia, and Elysse Nioupin - were interviewed comprehensively. Their answers are recorded throughout this document. This investigation was performed in accordance with MICRO-SOP-53, Sterility Test Out-of-Specification Investigation Procedure.\r \rUpon arrival, the sample was stored refrigerated in accordance with the Client's instructions. Prepping analyst - Ishita Sharma - and processing analyst - Alex Saravia - verified the physical integrity and packaging of the sample throughout both the preparation and processing stages. No leaks, cracks, or visible turbidity were observed at any point, verifying container integrity.\r \rAll reagents and supplies mentioned in the material section above were stored according to the suppliers' recommendations, and their integrity was visually verified before utilization. Moreover, all reagents and supplies had valid expiration dates. The functionality of all equipment was confirmed by reviewing data generated by our comprehensive in-house continuous monitoring system.\r \rThe cleanroom suite \(CR 114\) used for processing procedures comprises three interconnected sections: the innermost ISO 7 cleanroom \(114B\), which connects to the middle ISO 7 buffer room \(114A\), and then to the outermost ISO 8 anteroom \(114\). A positive air pressure system is maintained throughout the suite to ensure controlled, unidirectional airflow from 114B through 114A and into 114.\r \rThe ISO 5 Biological Safety Cabinet \(BSC\) E001316, located in the innermost ISO 7 room \(114B\), was used for sample processing steps. It was thoroughly cleaned and disinfected prior to each procedure in accordance with MICRO SOP-9 \(Cleaning and Disinfecting Procedure for Microbiology\). Additionally, BSC E001316 was certified and approved by both the Engineering and Quality Assurance teams. Sample processing was conducted in the ISO 5 BSC E001316 in the innermost ISO 7 room \(114B\) by Alex Saravia on 04Sep26 as per MICRO-SOP-5 \(USP <71> / EP 2.6.1 Sterility Test\).)/F 4/Subtype/Widget/Type/Annot/M(D:20250930132510-06'00')/DA(0 0 0 rg /Helv 0 Tf)/MK <<>>/FT/Tx/NM(2d4fc23c-8dc2-472e-9983-a92152ff6bdb)/AP <</N 900 0 R>>>>
endobj
315 0 obj
<</Ff 8392704/BS <</S/S/W 1>>/T(Text Field23)/Rect[66.481 71.956 554.681 567.478]/V(On 04Sep26, the sample vials for ETX-260902-0505 were received from the Sample Submissions team and brought into the Sterile Microbiology lab. Upon arrival, each sample vial was sprayed with an acidified bleach disinfectant, placed into pre-disinfected bins, and allowed a 10-minute contact time.\r \rThe secondary disinfection happened in the ISO 8 anteroom \(114\), where the vials were again treated with acidified bleach and provided a 10-minute contact time before processing. Subsequently, the vials were moved into innermost ISO 7 cleanroom \(114B\). Inside this cleanroom, the processing analyst, Alex Saravia, performed a final disinfection step, allowing an additional 10-minute contact time. Once fully disinfected, the vials were transferred into the ISO 5 BSC E001316.\r \rInside the BSC, each container was aseptically opened, ensuring minimal exposure to the environment. The contents were then dispensed directly from the container into sterile canisters placed on a vacuum manifold for membrane filtration \(45 gm filtered per media across 3 vials\). The sample was processed and tested as per MICRO-SOP-5 \(USP <71> / EP 2.6.1 Sterility Test\). Media bottles were transferred into incubators E001356 and E001357 to begin incubation.\r \rOn 10Sep26 \(Day 6 of incubation\), microbial growth was observed in one TSB \(Tryptic Soy Broth\) media bottle by analyst, Elysse Nioupin, and confirmed by Microbiology Lab Supervisor, Robin Seymour. The positive TSB bottle for ETX-260902-0505 was submitted for Differential Staining and Microbial Identification under ETX-260910-0290. Differential staining indicated the presence of Gram-positive rods. Definitive identification identified the isolate as Microbacterium sp. PM5 \(Gram-positive rods\).\r \rThe culture media utilized were within their expiry period. Negative control media bottles for the membrane filtration method were handled, processed, and incubated in the same manner as the test samples. No microbial growth was observed in the corresponding negative controls.\r \rEnvironmental Monitoring \(EM\) Recoveries Review:\r \rISO 5 BSC and Personnel Evaluation:\rUpon reviewing the environmental monitoring data associated with the sterility test, no microbial growth was detected on personnel monitoring plates \(left- and right-hand touch plates for analyst Alex Saravia\), settling plates, or ISO 5 BSC surface sampling plates within BSC E001316 on the date of testing \(04Sep26\), the preceding testing date \(03Sep26\), or the subsequent testing date \(08Sep26\). Therefore, the contemporaneous personnel and ISO 5 environmental monitoring results do not support processing personnel or the ISO 5 BSC as a potential source of the Microbacterium sp. PM5 detected in test sample ETX-260902-0505.\r \rCleanroom EM Evaluation:\rRoutine weekly surface sampling of Cleanroom Suite 114 performed on 04Sep26 demonstrated no microbial growth. Weekly active-air monitoring of the outermost ISO 8 Room 114 \(CR 114\) recovered 2 CFUs \(ETX-260914-0487\), identified as Corynebacterium ureicelerivorans and Mycobacterium grossiae. All of these cleanroom environmental isolates differed distinctly in genus and morphology from the product contamination isolate \(Microbacterium sp. PM5\). Considering the facility's layered disinfection program, controlled unidirectional airflow, physical segregation of the ISO 8 anteroom, and complete absence of contamination in the ISO 5 critical zone, these recoveries represent an isolated background event and are not considered a credible source of contamination for the test sample.\r \rThe absence of contamination on analyst glove plates and work-surface environmental monitoring plates collected on the date of testing indicates that there was no viable transfer of contaminants to the ISO 5 BSC. Additionally, the lack of contamination in other samples processed during the same testing session further demonstrates that the testing environment was operating in optimal condition.\r \rThe analysts confirmed full compliance with cleaning procedures as outlined in MICRO SOP-9 \(Cleaning and Disinfecting Procedure for Microbiology\) and MICRO-SOP-5 \(USP <71> / EP 2.6.1 Sterility Test\).\r \rMonthly cleaning and disinfection of the outermost ISO 8 Anteroom, the middle ISO 7 Buffer room, the innermost ISO 7 cleanroom, and its containing ISO 5 BSCs for Cleanroom suite CR114 was performed on 30Aug26, as per MICRO-SOP-9 \(Cleaning and Disinfecting Procedure for Microbiology\). During both cleaning cycles, it was documented that all H2O2 indicators passed. This confirms the efficient monthly cleaning of all three parts of Cleanroom suite CR114.\r \rAnalyzing a 6-month sample history for Solyn LLC \(E75000\), this specific analyte "GLP3R/Cagrilinitide" has had no prior failures using USP <71> / EP 2.6.1 Sterility Test during this period.\r \rTo assess the potential for sample-to-sample contamination contributing to the positive results, a comprehensive review was conducted of all samples processed on the same day. All other samples processed by the analyst and other analysts that day tested negative. These findings suggest that cross-contamination between samples is highly unlikely.\r \rBased on the observations outlined above, it is unlikely that the failing results were due to reagents, supplies, the cleanroom environment, the process, or analyst involvement. Consequently, the possibility of laboratory error contributing to this failure is minimal and the original result is deemed to be valid.\r \rHowever, when investigating the lot history, it was discovered that the same lot \(Lot 2608-216\) had previously been submitted for ScanRDI sterility testing under ETX-260807-0602 and yielded a passing result. Based on this conflicting passing ScanRDI result for the same lot, the laboratory requested additional sample vials from the client for retesting under ETX-260914-0470 and initiated a Phase II investigation \(CORP-FORM-22\).)/F 4/Subtype/Widget/Type/Annot/M(D:20250930132529-06'00')/DA(0 0 0 rg /Helv 0 Tf)/MK <<>>/FT/Tx/NM(141a6788-b002-4793-8d8c-ddc790ff63ba)/AP <</N 979 0 R>>>>
endobj
317 0 obj
<</Ff 8392704/BS <</S/S/W 1>>/T(Text Field24)/Rect[66.154 72.284 555.336 567.15]/V(PHASE II RETEST UNDER SUBMISSION ETX-260914-0470:\rIn accordance with Phase II investigation procedures under CORP-FORM-22, following the laboratory's discovery of the prior passing ScanRDI result \(ETX-260807-0602\) during the sample history investigation, additional sample vials from lot 2608-216 were requested from the client and submitted for retesting under submission ETX-260914-0470.\r \rThe retest sample was prepared by Andrew Carrillo on 14Sep26 and processed by Ayomide Odugbesi on 15Sep26 in accordance with MICRO-SOP-5 \(USP <71> / EP 2.6.1 Sterility Test\) via membrane filtration \(60 gm filtered across 4 vials\). The retest was conducted in certified ISO 5 BSC E001314 within Cleanroom Suite 115 following standard multi-barrier disinfection and strict aseptic technique. All culture media were within valid expiration dates. Media bottles were incubated in designated incubators E001356 and E001357. Negative controls were handled concurrently and remained sterile.\r \rOn 21Sep26 \(Day 06 of incubation\), microbial growth was observed in 1 x 100mL TSB media bottle by analyst Andrew Carrillo and confirmed by supervisor Robin Seymour. The positive bottle was submitted for Microbial Identification under ETX-260921-0498. Confirmatory identification definitively identified the isolate as Microbacterium sp. PM5 \(Gram-positive short rods\), matching the identical species and strain designation \(PM5\), morphology \(white shiny lawn growth\), media selection \(TSB\), and incubation kinetics \(Day 6\) of the initial failure under ETX-260902-0505 \(ETX-260910-0290\). All other samples processed in the batch on 15Sep26 tested negative.\r \rUpon analyzing environmental monitoring results for the retest processing on 15Sep26, no microbial growth was detected on operator personnel fingertip touch plates, settling plates, or ISO 5 BSC surface contact plates on the date of testing \(15Sep26\), the preceding testing date \(14Sep26\), or the subsequent testing date \(16Sep26\). Weekly active surface monitoring of Cleanroom Suite 115 showed no growth; weekly active air sampling in the outermost ISO 8 cleanroom 115 recovered 9 CFUs \(ETX-260923-0402\), identified as Kocuria indica, Brevibacterium sp. CS2, Corynebacterium sp, Micrococcus luteus, Paracoccus yeei, Staphylococcus hominis, and Kocuria rhizophila. All of these environmental isolates differed distinctly in genus and morphology from the product contamination isolate \(Microbacterium sp. PM5\). The ISO 5 critical zones in BSC E001314 remained completely sterile \(0 CFU\) and in optimal control throughout testing, with positive pressure cascades preventing anteroom air ingress. \(Refer to Table 1 for sample details, Table 2 for environmental monitoring from processing performed on 04Sep26, and Table 3 for environmental monitoring from processing performed on 15Sep26\).\r \rThe replication of microbial recovery in the independent retest - conducted by a different processing analyst \(Ayomide Odugbesi\) in a different cleanroom suite \(Cleanroom Suite 115 vs. Suite 114\) and a different prepping/reading analyst \(Andrew Carrillo\) - conclusively confirms that the microbial recovery was not an isolated laboratory anomaly, as the identical microorganism \(Microbacterium sp. PM5\) was recovered on the identical incubation day \(Day 6\) in the same media type \(TSB\) in both tests.\r \rThe discrepancy with the prior passing ScanRDI result \(ETX-260807-0602\) is attributed to non-uniform microbial distribution at low concentrations within the compounded batch. Consequently, inherent product contamination in Lot 2608-216 is confirmed, the initial failing result for ETX-260902-0505 is valid, retest ETX-260914-0470 is confirmed failing, and Lot 2608-216 fails sterility requirements.)/F 4/Subtype/Widget/Type/Annot/M(D:20250930132548-06'00')/DA(0 0 0 rg /Helv 0 Tf)/MK <<>>/FT/Tx/NM(c9f92eb7-3f54-41d3-86b9-c1e350482684)/AP <</N 1052 0 R>>>>
endobj
319 0 obj
<</Ff 8392704/BS <</S/S/W 1>>/T(Text Field25)/Rect[141.412 525.939 471.569 569.113]/V(Inherent product contamination in Lot 2608-216, confirmed by the replication of identical microbial recovery kinetics \(Day 6 in TSB\) and the identical strain \(Microbacterium sp. PM5\) in retest ETX-260914-0470. The discrepancy with the passing ScanRDI result \(ETX-260807-0602\) is scientifically attributed to non-uniform microbial distribution at low concentrations within the compounded batch. Original result confirmed valid.)/F 4/Subtype/Widget/Type/Annot/M(D:20250930132608-06'00')/DA(0 0 0 rg /Helv 0 Tf)/MK <<>>/FT/Tx/NM(b93c045e-3499-4749-96d7-2ac3aa1641f5)/AP <</N 1051 0 R>>>>
endobj
