<</Ff 8392704/BS <</S/S/W 1>>/Rect[66.154 72.284 555.336 567.15]/T(Text Field24)/F 4/V(PHASE II RETEST UNDER SUBMISSION ETX-260914-0470:\rIn accordance with Phase II investigation procedures under CORP-FORM-22, following the laboratory's discovery of the prior passing ScanRDI result \(ETX-260807-0602\) during the sample history investigation, additional sample vials from lot 2608-216 were requested from the client and submitted for retesting under submission ETX-260914-0470.\r \rThe retest sample was prepared by Andrew Carrillo on 14Sep26 and processed by Ayomide Odugbesi on 15Sep26 in accordance with MICRO-SOP-5 \(USP <71> / EP 2.6.1 Sterility Test\) via membrane filtration \(60 gm filtered across 4 vials\). The retest was conducted in certified ISO 5 BSC E001314 within Cleanroom Suite 115 following comparable disinfection and strict aseptic technique to original testing. All culture media were within valid expiration dates. Media bottles were incubated in designated incubators E001356 and E001357. Negative controls were handled concurrently and remained sterile.\r \rOn 21Sep26 \(Day 06 of incubation\), microbial growth was observed in 1 x 100mL TSB media bottle by analyst Andrew Carrillo and confirmed by supervisor Robin Seymour. The positive bottle was submitted for Microbial Identification under ETX-260921-0498. Confirmatory identification definitively identified the isolate as Microbacterium sp. PM5 \(Gram-positive short rods\), matching the identical species and strain designation \(PM5\), morphology \(white shiny lawn growth\), media selection \(TSB\), and incubation kinetics \(Day 6\) of the initial failure under ETX-260902-0505 \(ETX-260910-0290\). All other samples processed in the batch on 15Sep26 tested negative.\r \rUpon analyzing environmental monitoring results for the retest processing on 15Sep26, no microbial growth was detected on operator personnel fingertip touch plates, settling plates, or ISO 5 BSC surface contact plates on the date of testing \(15Sep26\), the preceding testing date \(14Sep26\), or the subsequent testing date \(16Sep26\). Weekly active surface monitoring of Cleanroom Suite 115 showed no growth; weekly active air sampling in the outermost ISO 8 cleanroom 115 recovered 9 CFUs \(ETX-260923-0402\), identified as Kocuria indica, Brevibacterium sp. CS2, Corynebacterium sp, Micrococcus luteus, Paracoccus yeei, Staphylococcus hominis, and Kocuria rhizophila. All of these environmental isolates differed distinctly in genus and morphology from the product contamination isolate \(Microbacterium sp. PM5\). The ISO 5 critical zones in BSC E001314 remained completely sterile \(0 CFU\) and in optimal control throughout testing, with positive pressure cascades preventing anteroom air ingress. \(Refer to Table 1 for sample details, Table 2 for environmental monitoring from processing performed on 04Sep26, and Table 3 for environmental monitoring from processing performed on 15Sep26\).\r \rIt was identified that the same sample lot \(2608-216\) had previously been submitted by the client for ScanRDI sterility testing under submission ETX-260807-0602 and yielded passing results. However, it is important to note that a review of the submission determined that no approved method suitability test was available to support the validity for microbial growth recovery using the ScanRDI method for this specific sample. \r \rFurthermore, the discrepancy between the previous passing ScanRDI result and the current USP <71> sterility failure may be attributable to the non-homogeneous distribution of microbial contamination within the compounded batch. When microbial contamination is present at very low levels, microorganisms may not be uniformly distributed throughout the product, resulting in variability between samples collected from the same lot. Consequently, one aliquot may not contain contaminating microorganisms and yield an acceptable result, while another aliquot from the same batch may contain viable microorganisms and generate a positive sterility test result. Given the absence of a qualified method suitability study for the ScanRDI submission, the prior passing result cannot be considered directly comparable to the current USP <71> sterility test outcome.\r \rHowever, the replication of microbial recovery in the independent retest - conducted by a different processing analyst \(Ayomide Odugbesi\) in a different cleanroom suite \(Cleanroom Suite 115 vs. Suite 114\) and a different prepping/reading analyst \(Andrew Carrillo\) - conclusively confirms that the microbial recovery was not an isolated laboratory anomaly, as the identical microorganism \(Microbacterium sp. PM5\) was recovered on the identical incubation day \(Day 6\) in the same media type \(TSB\) in both tests. \r \rTherefore, inherent product contamination in Lot 2608-216 is confirmed, both the initial failing result for ETX-260902-0505 and the retest ETX-260914-0470 is considiered valid. )/Type/Annot/Subtype/Widget/DA(0 0 0 rg /Helv 0 Tf)/M(D:20250930132548-06'00')/FT/Tx/MK <<>>/AP <</N 1065 0 R>>/NM(c9f92eb7-3f54-41d3-86b9-c1e350482684)>>
endobj
